<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      platform_admin_audit — the platform operator's own trail
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      WHY IT HAS TO EXIST HERE AND NOT IN audit_db

      Every mutation this milestone adds to the platform user/RBAC surface is an action taken by a
      platform_users row against a tenant's data. The obvious home for that record is
      audit_db.audit_events, and it does not fit, for three independent reasons that were
      established from source before this table was proposed:

        1. audit_events.tenant_id is NOT NULL and its actor columns are read as auth_db.users ids
           by every consumer. A platform_users id written into user_id names somebody who does not
           exist in that tenant — or, worse, coincidentally does. That is the D-34 defect
           (impersonation rows recording their target as their own actor) re-created one layer up.
        2. platform_db cannot reach audit_db AT ALL. Separate databases, zero cross-grants in all
           14 tenant databases, and platform_db has only plpgsql installed — no postgres_fdw, no
           dblink (measured in 040-platform-db-rls-posture.xml). There is no SQL bridge.
        3. audit_events and every one of its partitions are FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, and the
           platform plane has no tenant GUC to populate. audit-service's /internal/audit/events
           looks like a door and is very likely inert for exactly that reason — it has no test and
           nothing on that path sets the GUC. Building a control on it would be building on a
           measurement nobody has taken.

      So the platform tier keeps its trail where it can actually write it and actually read it,
      beside impersonation_log, which is the same kind of record for the same reason.

      WHAT THIS TABLE IS NOT

      It is not a replacement for the tenant-side event. A platform-tier deactivation still
      publishes USER_DEACTIVATED into the tenant's own outbox, so the tenant sees what happened to
      its account. What that event deliberately does NOT carry is a platform id in an actor column
      (see UserLifecycleEventPublisher.platformActivationChanged). This row is the other half: who.
      Neither is a substitute for the other, and a reader wanting the whole picture needs both.

      Two actions here publish NO tenant event at all and this is their only record —
      USER_UNLOCK and USER_SESSIONS_REVOKED. There is no USER_UNLOCKED type in
      AuditEventCatalog.MUST_AUDIT and adding one is a shared-lib change rippling through sixteen
      services and a build-enforced allow-list closure test, for two operations that grant nothing
      and revoke nothing. Recorded here as a KNOWN ASYMMETRY rather than papered over: a tenant
      reading only its own audit_events will not see a platform-cleared lockout.

      RLS: NONE, deliberately — 040 is the decision record and its reasoning applies unchanged.
      The console has no tenant claim, so the fleet-standard policy would take this table to zero
      rows and 42501 on every insert. Isolation is by database and by role.

      APPEND-ONLY, by trigger and not by grant, for the reason 040-002 measured at length: on this
      cluster platform_user inherits platform_admin, so a REVOKE is INERT and a trigger is the only
      layer that fires for the owner, for an inheriting member, and for Liquibase. Unlike
      impersonation_log there is no carve-out at all here — no column of this table is ever
      legitimately updated, because the row describes something that already happened.
    -->

    <changeSet id="050-001-create-platform-admin-audit" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><tableExists tableName="platform_admin_audit"/></not>
        </preConditions>

        <createTable tableName="platform_admin_audit">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="occurred_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="NOW()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!--
              The acting platform_users.id, taken from the sub of an RS256-verified control-plane
              token. NOT NULL and no default: an audit row that cannot say who is one somebody has
              to interpret rather than read, and a placeholder would name a person who did not act.
              No FK to platform_users — the trail must outlive the account, and a cascade or a
              restrict on an accountability record is a way to lose it or a way to be unable to
              remove a leaver.
            -->
            <column name="platform_user_id" type="UUID">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!--
              Denormalised on purpose. The id is authoritative; the address is what an abuse review
              reads the row BY, and resolving it at read time against a table whose rows may since
              have been rotated (910 deactivates and replaces the seeded SuperAdmin) produces a
              trail that changes its own history. Same call audit_events makes for targetEmail.
            -->
            <column name="platform_user_email" type="VARCHAR(255)"/>
            <column name="action" type="VARCHAR(60)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!--
              Nullable: not every platform action is tenant-scoped, and a synthetic tenant id would
              be a lie. No FK, for the same reason as platform_user_id above.
            -->
            <column name="tenant_id" type="UUID"/>
            <column name="target_user_id" type="UUID"/>
            <!--
              Mandatory at the application layer for every mutation. A reset used to lock a rival
              out is indistinguishable from a legitimate one without it (T-13-13-E). Nullable in the
              column only so that a future non-mutating record type is not forced to invent one.
            -->
            <column name="reason" type="VARCHAR(500)"/>
            <column name="outcome" type="VARCHAR(20)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!--
              Plain-language "what actually happened" — 'sessionsRevoked=3', 'active=false',
              'upstream refused: ROLE_CEILING_EXCEEDED'. Never a credential: the platform reset
              returns a temporary password to the operator and it exists nowhere else, and this
              column must never become the place it is written down. There is no structured
              before/after here because this service does not hold the rows it is acting on.
            -->
            <column name="detail" type="VARCHAR(1000)"/>
        </createTable>

        <sql>ALTER TABLE platform_admin_audit ADD CONSTRAINT chk_platform_admin_audit_outcome
            CHECK (outcome IN ('SUCCEEDED','REFUSED'))</sql>

        <!--
          The action vocabulary, constrained in the database rather than only in the enum. A typo'd
          or renamed constant then fails the INSERT loudly instead of writing a row that no query
          filters on — the drift class this repository has hit repeatedly over permission codes.
          Adding an action means a changeset, which is the intended amount of friction for widening
          what the platform tier may do.
        -->
        <sql>ALTER TABLE platform_admin_audit ADD CONSTRAINT chk_platform_admin_audit_action
            CHECK (action IN (
                'USER_PASSWORD_RESET',
                'USER_DEACTIVATED',
                'USER_REACTIVATED',
                'USER_UNLOCKED',
                'USER_SESSIONS_REVOKED'))</sql>

        <!-- Newest-first is the only order this table is ever read in. -->
        <createIndex tableName="platform_admin_audit" indexName="idx_paa_occurred_at">
            <column name="occurred_at" descending="true"/>
        </createIndex>
        <!-- "Where has operator X been?" — the question this table exists to answer. -->
        <createIndex tableName="platform_admin_audit" indexName="idx_paa_operator">
            <column name="platform_user_id"/>
            <column name="occurred_at" descending="true"/>
        </createIndex>
        <!-- "What has the platform done to MY tenant?" -->
        <createIndex tableName="platform_admin_audit" indexName="idx_paa_tenant">
            <column name="tenant_id"/>
            <column name="occurred_at" descending="true"/>
        </createIndex>

        <sql>
            COMMENT ON TABLE platform_admin_audit IS
            'Accountability record of platform operator (SuperAdmin) actions on tenant users. NO RLS BY DESIGN — see 040-platform-db-rls-posture.xml; the console token carries no tenant_id so the fleet tenant_isolation policy would match ZERO rows. APPEND-ONLY, enforced by trg_platform_admin_audit_immutable + trg_platform_admin_audit_no_truncate; the accompanying REVOKE is INERT because platform_user inherits platform_admin. This is the ONLY record of USER_UNLOCKED and USER_SESSIONS_REVOKED — those publish no tenant-side event. For USER_DEACTIVATED / USER_REACTIVATED / USER_PASSWORD_RESET the tenant also has its own audit_events row, which deliberately carries NO platform id in any actor column.';
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <dropTable tableName="platform_admin_audit"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="050-002-platform-admin-audit-append-only-grants" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <!--
          Layer 1, and READ 040-002 BEFORE TRUSTING IT: on this cluster platform_user inherits
          platform_admin, so this REVOKE changes the catalogue view and nothing else. It is kept
          because it is correct and becomes load-bearing the day ownership is fixed; it is NOT a
          second enforcing layer today. Layer 2 is.

          Guarded on the role existing: under Testcontainers the container superuser is the only
          role present, and a REVOKE naming a role that does not exist is an ERROR that would abort
          Liquibase and fail every IT in this service at context startup.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            BEGIN
                IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'platform_user') THEN
                    REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON platform_admin_audit FROM platform_user;
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <sql>REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON platform_admin_audit FROM PUBLIC;</sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="false">
                DO $$
                BEGIN
                    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'platform_user') THEN
                        GRANT UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON platform_admin_audit TO platform_user;
                    END IF;
                END $$;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="050-003-platform-admin-audit-immutability-trigger" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <!--
          Layer 2 — fires for EVERY role including the table owner and the migration tool. Same
          shape as 040-003 and audit-service's 011.

          No UPDATE carve-out at all, unlike impersonation_log's ended_at. Nothing about a completed
          action is subsequently corrected: if an operator did something else afterwards, that is
          another row. A guard with an exception is a guard somebody eventually widens.

          A row-level trigger does not see TRUNCATE — TRUNCATE bypasses row triggers entirely — so
          the statement-level one below is what stops the whole trail being erased in one statement.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION prevent_platform_admin_audit_rewrite() RETURNS trigger AS $$
            BEGIN
                RAISE EXCEPTION
                    'PLATFORM_ADMIN_AUDIT_IMMUTABLE: platform operator audit rows cannot be updated or deleted — this is the accountability trail for SuperAdmin actions on tenant accounts';
            END;
            $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
        </sql>

        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION prevent_platform_admin_audit_truncate() RETURNS trigger AS $$
            BEGIN
                RAISE EXCEPTION
                    'PLATFORM_ADMIN_AUDIT_IMMUTABLE: platform_admin_audit cannot be truncated — a row-level trigger does not see TRUNCATE, so this statement-level one exists to stop the whole trail being erased in one statement';
            END;
            $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
        </sql>

        <!-- DROP before CREATE so a re-run against a partially-applied database is a no-op. -->
        <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_platform_admin_audit_immutable ON platform_admin_audit;</sql>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE TRIGGER trg_platform_admin_audit_immutable
                BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ON platform_admin_audit
                FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION prevent_platform_admin_audit_rewrite();
        </sql>

        <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_platform_admin_audit_no_truncate ON platform_admin_audit;</sql>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE TRIGGER trg_platform_admin_audit_no_truncate
                BEFORE TRUNCATE ON platform_admin_audit
                FOR EACH STATEMENT EXECUTE FUNCTION prevent_platform_admin_audit_truncate();
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_platform_admin_audit_immutable ON platform_admin_audit;</sql>
            <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_platform_admin_audit_no_truncate ON platform_admin_audit;</sql>
            <sql>DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS prevent_platform_admin_audit_rewrite();</sql>
            <sql>DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS prevent_platform_admin_audit_truncate();</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
